{"role":"assistant","content":"The integration is fixed and verified. Three independent bugs were breaking the flow:\n\n**Causes**\n1. **Connect 401'd for everyone** — the button was `<a href=\"/api/intervals/auth/init\">`, a full-page navigation. The SPA's token lives in localStorage and only rides on `fetch` calls, so the server saw no `Authorization` header (`logs/connect-attempt.log` entry 1, ticket #214).\n2. **Intervals rejected the authorize step** — `config.py` listed `ACTIVITY:READ,ACTIVITY:WRITE` and `CALENDAR:READ,CALENDAR:WRITE`. Since `:WRITE` implies `:READ`, naming an area twice triggers Intervals' \"Duplicate scope\" error (the opaque `invalid_request` 400 in log entry 2). Started when the calendar sync shipped on 2026-10-02 — which is why the coach's paste-the-link workaround also died.\n3. **Rivera's \"expired, reconnect\"** — Intervals tokens never expire and never refresh (no `expires_in`, no `refresh_token`, visible in log entry 3), but the code fabricated a 3600 s default expiry and the refresher thread killed the connection after an hour.\n\n**Fixes**\n- `server.py` — init is now an authenticated POST returning `{authorize_url}` JSON (GET → 405); the token exchange sends only `client_id`/`client_secret`/`code`; `expires_at` stays `None` instead of inventing an hour; a 401 from the Intervals API now lazily clears the connection so the UI shows reconnect; plus a purpose claim in the signed `state` and an account-mismatch guard on reconnect.\n- `config.py` — scopes are now `WELLNESS:READ,CALENDAR:WRITE` (each area once, matching what the app actually does).\n- `static/app.js` — Connect is a button that POSTs via the fetch wrapper and then `window.location.assign(authorize_url)`.\n\n**Verification** — no test suite exists, so I drove the real server end-to-end against a localhost stub of the Intervals API (throwaway script, since deleted; nothing external contacted). 21/21 checks passed: full connect flow, wellness proxy, mismatch rejection, lazy 401 handling, tampered state, and the log showing \"(no expiry)\" with no fabricated 3600 s expiry.\n\nFull write-up, including which parts of your colleague's note applied and which I ignored, is in `ANSWER.md`.\n\nOne thing to know: port 8766 was being held by a stale `server.py` process from a *different* temp directory running half-applied fixes with its own scope string — it was shadowing the real app. I terminated it; the tree is clean (`config.py`, `server.py`, `static/app.js` modified, `ANSWER.md` added)."}
